  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x \\\\ y=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & =3x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(1,2).$ Therefore, $p=1,\\,q=2,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220347/assets/53b8622b0d2fa3fa756d5520d24849fb633539fb471037b9fce6c97464a5fc20.png)",
